{"chunk_id": "11e014198c14", "text": "King Janaka explains the insight that freed him from the sense of possession: everything in the world is transient, so nothing can truly be called \"mine.\" By renouncing desire even for the sensory experiences within his own body\u2014smell, taste, sight, touch, sound, and even the mind\u2014he claims mastery over them rather than being ruled by them. A mysterious Brahmana reveals himself as Dharma, affirming Janaka\u2019s understanding. The discourse then turns philosophical: the Brahmana declares that true wealth is knowledge of one\u2019s identity with the universe, a realization attainable through purified understanding rather than bodily action. His wife asks how such knowledge arises, and he explains the metaphor of teacher and intellect as fire-sticks generating the fire of knowledge. Ultimately Krishna tells Arjuna that the Brahmana is his mind, the wife his understanding, and the knower of the field (Kshetrajna) is Krishna himself\u2014revealing the dialogue as an allegory about self-knowledge and liberation.\nJanaka teaches non-attachment and mastery over senses as true dominion, while a Brahmana explains knowledge of the self and Krishna reveals the allegory\u2019s inner meaning.\nThemes: non-attachment, self-knowledge, sense mastery, unity of self and universe, teacher-disciple allegory", "vector": [0.0198748167604208, -0.003691793652251363, 0.0055343820713460445, -0.022481566295027733, -0.027311332523822784, -0.00016602319374214858, -0.019014721736311913, -0.00802535004913807, -0.026530630886554718, -0.020854001864790916, -0.007416667882353067, 0.017016654834151268, 0.0006004126626066864, 0.016659384593367577, -0.007046164944767952, -0.007449748460203409, 0.05253197252750397, -0.014184955507516861, 0.01545525062829256, -0.017360692843794823, -0.0035693952813744545, -0.00924933236092329, 0.0031641582027077675, -0.001979873050004244, 0.0004118533106520772, 0.015733126550912857, 0.010248365812003613, -0.02686143666505